2026-09-18 02:56:42,344 [INFO] excalibur.core.langfuse: [TOOL]: Bash: {'command': '# Wait - nmap said 256 hosts up. That\'s interesting. Let me re-check.\n# All 256 IPs show as "up" but only .1 shows open ports. This could be due to ARP behavior.\n# Let\'s try RIP route injection using scapy.\n\npython3 << \'PYEOF\'\nfrom scapy.all import *\n\n# We need to send RIP request to the router and see the response\n# RIP v2 uses UDP port 520, multicast 224.0.0.9\n\n# First, let\'s send a RIP request to the router\nrip_request = IP(dst="10.254.200.1")/UDP(sport=520, dport=520)/Raw(load=b\'\\x01\\x01\\x00\\x00\' + b\'\\x00\\x00\' * 10)\n# RIP command 1 (request), version 1, with one entry (AFI 0, metric 16 = full table request)\n\nprint("Sending RIP request...")\n# Use sr1 for response\ntry:\n    resp = sr1(rip_request, timeout=3, verbose=1)\n    if resp:\n        print(f"Response: {resp.summary()}")\n        if Raw in resp:\n            print(f"Raw data: {resp[Raw].load.hex()}")\n    else:\n        print("No response to RIP request")\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f"Error: {e}")\n\n# Also try RIP v2\nrip_v2_req = IP(dst="224.0.0.9")/UDP(sport=520, dport=520)/Raw(load=b\'\\x01\\x02\\x00\\x00\' + b\'\\x00\\x00\' * 10)\nprint("\\nSending RIP v2 request to multicast...")\ntry:\n    resp = sr1(rip_v2_req, timeout=3, verbose=1)\n    if resp:\n        print(f"Response: {resp.summary()}")\n        if Raw in resp:\n            print(f"Raw data: {resp[Raw].load.hex()}")\n    else:\n        print("No response to RIP v2 request")\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f"Error: {e}")\n\nPYEOF', 'description': 'Send RIP requests via Scapy to discover routes', 'timeout': 30000}; start
